James sought advice on how to cut and shorten a thick leather strap, considering using grommets. I advised against grommets due to their structural limitations and proposed various stronger alternatives: buckle-style shortening, overlap with rivets, butt joint with a backer piece, and stitching. Upon deciding to use rivets, James inquired about the necessary tools. I outlined essential tools for making rivet holes, such as a leather hole punch, cutting surface, mallet, and appropriate rivet setters. I suggested a minimal setup for basic tasks or a more comprehensive leatherworking kit for future projects. I provided a straightforward step-by-step guide for using double-cap rivets, along with tips on estimating hole sizes and selecting rivet lengths based on leather thickness.
